let's talk about the government's  flagship welfare reform universal credit  last week on this show the Labour Party  position changed from pause and fix to  scrapping it altogether but benefit  combines six separate payments into one  and aims to ensure that work pays and  people don't become trapped on welfare  and while the concept has been welcomed  largely execution has had a few teething  problems well joining us to discuss all  of this is the shadow attorney-general  baroness Chakrabarti and thanks for  being with us and I want to start off  with Universal Credit and last week John  MacDonald the Shadow Chancellor said  that it will have to go effectively  saying that Labor's position would be to  get rid of it and what I'm a little bit  unclear about is what you'd like to  replace it with well can I just say I  know you described the position as  changing from pause and fix to scrapping  I understand that dramatic language will  make you think that this is a complete  change of policy but in the end people  need to be supported when they're out of  work when they're on low paid work they  need to be supported in a number of ways  including via benefits so whether you  end up changing the name of a benefit or  changing the structure of a benefit in  the end what's important is the end of  austerity and people having the money  that they need to live to stay alive in  the sixth wealthiest economy on earth so  are you saying that labor might not  scrap Universal Credit what I'm saying  is that I don't think Universal Credit  in its current form is sustainable and  that's why we're urging the government  to to stop the rollout now because it's  causing such hardship to people the idea  the theoretical idea that you could have  one simple benefit rather than people  having to apply for lots of benefits  always appeal to me in a theory the  problem with the Universal Credit as it  now is under this government is that  it's being used to cut people's benefits  to penalize people for having too many  children to penalize the disabled and  other vulnerable people to be the notion  of a simple simpler benefit system great  idea but not if you're adding austerity  to it so if you're saying are you saying  then that if enough money was put into  universal credit you'd actually be happy  to see it today well I personally I  think the term Universal Credit is now  such a toxic brand the  would be very hard to rebuild trust in  it but we are talking with the people  who've been affected we're talking with  the charities who have been trying to to  paper over the cracks and support some  of the most vulnerable people in society  including via food banks we're working  with them talking with them to try and  work out the most practical way to make  things better and if you contrast what  we're trying to do working with civil  society with what the government's doing  including gagging clauses into contracts  that the government has with with  charities and other third parties I  think that's a very very different  approach the gating clauses in  particular something that you're  concerned well you know as a shadow  attorney you can imagine and with a  human rights and free speech background  you can you can imagine that was pretty  shocked to learn of that contracts  between the DWP that gesture mcveigh's  Department and charities include clauses  that preclude the charities from  criticizing the Secretary of State the  department or Universal Credit that's  completely unacceptable  and I think quite possibly unenforceable  in our court so margaret Greenwood has  written to estimate they urging her to  come to Parliament and make very  publicly clear that these these gagging  clauses will not be enforced and they  will not continue in any any new  contracts between government and third  parties  now lots of things to discuss them on  the program and before we get to some of  the other issues I am keen to talk to  you about anti-semitism because you of  course authored the report into  anti-semitism over the summer we've had  and several stories allegations coming  forward and about alleged anti-semitism  in the Labor Party so I'm quite keen to  talk to you about some of those with  your report for reference so I think we  can look at some thing that you wrote in  your report which is this and it's  always a sundry to compare the actions  of Jewish people or institutions to  those of Hitler or the Nazis now in 2013  Jeremy Corbyn it emerged over the summer  and said that Palestinians in the West  Bank live under occupation of the very  sort that would be recognized by many  people in Europe who suffered occupation  during the Second World War so by your  definition that would be in century was  that like what incendiary by the way  doesn't mean an  he submitted no quite so let me put it  like this by the time I was looking at  the nature of discourse within the party  by definition we had a problem that that  needed to be addressed and I was trying  to set down some guidelines for people  who want to you know to make very  important arguments but not make them in  a way that is going to just  automatically get people's backs and I  understand I don't I know this is before  you report yeah would you say those  comments were incensed I think that I  stand by my advice to everybody which is  to make your human rights critiques and  and Jeremy like me as a lifelong human  rights defender and he is quite right to  draw people's attention to the plight of  the Palestinians but I think that it is  better if possible given the rise of the  far right given the rise of  anti-semitism in Europe is better to  make those human rights arguments  without comparison to to the Nazis and  now in another section you talk about  Jewish stereotyping and anti-semitic  conspiracy tropes and you say and this  about that I'm not saying that this is  endemic but any seasoned activist who  says that they are completely unaware of  any such discourse must be wholly  insensitive or completely in denial and  and I just want to talk to you about  Peter wills been reelected to the  National Executive Committee and who was  recorded as saying that he's never seen  any anti-semitism in the party so it's  the insensitive or in denial well I  don't know but I stand by when I said  which is there's been a real issue and I  do understand that some people will say  that they've not personally experienced  it well guess what if you're not Jewish  you're not going to necessarily  personally experienced it and maybe you  have been insensitive to it or you know  maybe it's just you're speaking for your  own personal experience but I think  about racism of any kind and or frankly  misogyny or any other kind of  discrimination or hey the thing about it  is that you've got to try and listen to  the broader experience you've got to try  to understand things that that you've  not experienced yourself  trying to that was what I was trying to  advocate then and continue to advocating  that would you feel more comfortable he  hadn't been reelected to the executive  committee um possibly you know but but  he has apologized and people have to  have the opportunity to reflect and  learn and change and be more sensitive  and become aware of other people's  experiences as Jeremy Corbyn said at  conference and by the way that was a  conference of you know it sort of 15,000  people who came together in this  democratic moment and generally speaking  behaved quite well with each other he  said we need to learn to listen more and  shout less and that I think is it is a  crucial point not just not just in the  labour party but in British politics and  politics more generally that's what I  would advocate and now your course leave  shadow giant Attorney General so justice  and completely Affairs if you like your  brief and so I'm interested to get your  thoughts on the case of Karen white who  is the transgender prisoner convicted  rapist who was moved into a women's  prison where she then committed sexual  assault now she's been sentenced now to  life in prison should she spend the rest  of her sentence in a male prison or a  female prison do you think so here's my  position on the trans woman issue I do  believe in self-identification but  nonetheless there are some people who  should not be incarcerated together now  there's a wealth of case law from the  Court of Human Rights in Strasbourg for  example about racists who have been put  in prison cells with members of minority  communities now they might be of the  same sex and therefore they're in the  right prison you would say but those two  people should never have been put in a  Cell together because that would make  one inherently vulnerable so it's more  about her previous convictions rather  about the gender the resident I think  that I think there are some people who  should not be housed together whether in  a refuge whether in a woman's refuge or  whether in a woman's prison or an other  vulnerable  so should the Ministry of Justice policy  be updated then in a minute it is saying  that transgender prisoners should be  located in the part of the estate  consistent with the gender they identify  I think that you've just got to be more  specific than that some people are a  danger to other particular people and  they should not be housed or put in  refuge or put in prison together these  are a minute it's a common-sense  administrative practical responsible  solutions to people's vulnerability it's  not about whether a trans woman is a  woman like me or a woman like you it's  it's much more specific I think and  before the interview in Tacoma also very  keen to get your thoughts on Jamal  khashoggi who is the journalist who has  not been seen after entering the Saudi  consulate and in Turkey very concerning  story clearly and what do you think the  UK government needs to do now I think we  need to be much more robust and Emily  Thornbury our shadow foreign secretary  is written in The Observer today a very  robust piece about how we need to start  calling out Saudi Arabia I'm not saying  that it's just the Conservative  government you know Robin Cook was  heartbroken about not being able to  follow through on ethical foreign policy  largely because of things like arms  sales to the Middle East and we've got  to we've got to stop being more  consistent in our criticisms of of other  governments and as Emily's written today  you know would the government be quite  so reticent if this kind of behavior  were being conducted by Iran Russia  other countries possibly not and so are  you talking about like stopping Armour  sales sanctions what potential  potentially I mean um you know we cannot  be arming Saudi Arabia without some  without real assurances that those arms  are not being used against innocent  civilians in Yemen against dissidents  and Saudi Arabia itself you know Saudi  Arabia should be in the dock when it  comes to human rights abuses including  women's rights abuses on this planet and  we need to be more consistent in our  attitude to human rights okay Baroness  check our body language

